.TH FBMODE 8
.SH NAME
fbmode \- show or change the mode of the screen
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBfbmode\fR [\fB\-d\fR \fIdevice\fR] [\fIwidth\fBx\fIheight\fR[\fB@\fIhz\fR]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Without an argument, \fBfbmode\fR lists the modes the screen can be given,
one a line, as \fIwidth\fBx\fIheight\fB@\fIhz\fR; the one on the screen is
marked \fBcurrent\fR.
The list is the frame buffer driver's: the modes the monitor reports
(EDID, its CEA-861 extension included) that the driver can make, or, on
a virtual display, the driver's own few.
.PP
With an argument, \fBfbmode\fR changes the screen to that mode: the one of
that size whose rate is nearest to \fIhz\fR, or to 60 if \fIhz\fR is not
given.
It prints the mode taken.
The console on the screen takes the new size at once, keeping its lines
up to the cursor.
.PP
While a program such as the compositor has the screen, the driver refuses
the change; the compositor changes the mode itself
(\fBmeechowm \-m mode\fR \fIwidth\fBx\fIheight\fR).
.PP
The mode at boot is chosen by the driver: the monitor's preferred, or the
largest it lists that the driver makes; the boot parameter
\fBfbmode=\fIwidth\fBx\fIheight\fR[\fB@\fIhz\fR] asks for another.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP 10
\fB\-d\fR \fIdevice\fR
The frame buffer; \fB/dev/fb0\fR by default.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
0 on success; 1 if the driver refused or the device did not open; 2 for
an argument that is not a mode.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
meechowm(1)
